# Timbda-0 differential corpus, M0 slice (docs/PLAN.md sec. 6 "Differential harness").
#
# This file is the harness's single specification: one case = one closed kernel
# expression, the head-table rule its root must select, and the normal form the
# evaluator must reach.  Two corners read this file independently and must agree:
#   - tools/corpus/corpus.py  reads it and emits the Coq corner: Coq's own
#     `Timbda.Eval.norm` / `head_redex` asserted by `reflexivity`;
#   - tools/corpus/driver.ml  reads it a second time, independently, and runs the
#     *extracted* OCaml evaluator (tools/extract/extract.v -> timbda_eval.ml).
# The `norm` expectations below were derived by hand from the paper rules (Figs.
# 9-13, transcribed in docs/VERSE-RULES.md) in the kernel's documented evaluation
# order.  See tools/corpus/README.md for the strategy, and for what this corpus
# does and does not establish.
#
# Grammar (canonical, no sugar; the desugaring table is in tools/corpus/README.md):
#   expr := (ret val) | (glob POS) | (app expr expr) | (tup expr ...)
#         | (seq expr expr) | (unify expr expr) | (choice expr expr) | fail
#         | (block (slot ...) expr) | (iter comb expr expr)
#   slot := (ty) | (ty val)                     # declared-only, or filled
#   val  := unit | true | false | INT | (primv prim) | (tupv val ...)
#         | (lam ty expr) | (delay expr) | (var N)
#   ty   := tunit | tbool | tint | (arr ty ty) | tany
#   comb := iall | iif | ifor
# INT is a Z literal and may be negative; (var N) is a nat index; (glob POS) is
# a positive literal (Syntax.v: ident := positive, Glob : ident -> Expr).

defaults fuel 24

# ---------- Fig. 12: structural / normalization rows ---------------------------

case seq_ret
  head r_seq
  expr (seq (ret unit) (ret 1))
  norm (ret 1)
  why "Seq: seq of a value drops the left component (RewSound_Seq)."

case norm5_reassoc
  head r_norm5
  expr (seq (seq fail fail) fail)
  norm (seq fail (seq fail fail))
  why "Norm5 re-associates a nested seq; the re-associated root has no rule (neither side is a value), so the one-pass structural rebuild is the normal form."

case norm1_value_through_seq
  head r_norm1
  expr (unify (ret unit) (seq fail fail))
  norm (seq fail (unify (ret unit) fail))
  why "Norm1 pushes a value through a seq; `unify unit fail` has no row (the right side is not a value)."

case norm2_distribution
  head r_norm2
  expr (unify (ret unit) (unify fail fail))
  norm (seq (unify (ret unit) fail) (unify (ret unit) fail))
  why "Norm2 distributes a value over a nested unify (distribution is a seq of unifications, not a unify)."

case norm3_seq_left
  head r_norm3
  expr (unify (seq fail fail) fail)
  norm (seq fail (unify fail fail))
  why "Norm3 pulls the left seq out of a unify."

case norm4_nested_unify_value
  head r_norm4
  expr (unify (unify (ret unit) fail) fail)
  norm (seq (unify (ret unit) fail) (unify (ret unit) fail))
  why "Norm4: value on the left of a nested unify - same distribution shape as Norm2."

case norm1_then_seq_drops_the_value
  head r_norm1
  expr (unify (ret 3) (seq (ret unit) fail))
  norm (unify (ret 3) fail)
  why "Norm1 pushes the value through the seq, giving `unit ; (3 = fail)`; the head position IS re-visited (head steps consume fuel one at a time), so R_Seq then drops the `ret unit`, and `3 = fail` takes no row because its right side is not a value. Contrast eqtup1_single_element, where the rebuild happens in the structural pass and is therefore not re-visited."

# ---------- Fig. 9: unification taxonomy --------------------------------------

case eqk1_int
  head r_eqk1
  expr (unify (ret 42) (ret 42))
  norm (ret 42)
  why "EqK1: equal literals unify to the literal."

case eqk1_unit
  head r_eqk1
  expr (unify (ret unit) (ret unit))
  norm (ret unit)
  why "EqK1 for unit (is_lit_val covers unit/true/false/int, Fig. 6 line 320)."

case eqk2_int_unequal
  head r_eqk2
  expr (unify (ret 1) (ret 2))
  norm fail
  why "EqK2: unequal literals are fail, not stuck."

case eqk2_unit_vs_int
  head r_eqk2
  expr (unify (ret unit) (ret 3))
  norm fail
  why "unit vs 3 is a *literal* pair (both sides take the EqK1/EqK2 branch), not EqFail - the same distinction KernelTest.v documents."

case eqtup1_single_element
  head r_eqtup1
  expr (unify (ret (tupv unit)) (ret (tupv unit)))
  norm (seq (ret unit) (ret (tupv unit)))
  why "EqTup1 builds `x=x ; angle-bracket-v` (eq_seq, line 435); the inner unify reduces in the structural pass and the outer seq needs a further fuel pass (README sec. 3)."

case eqtup1_two_elements
  head r_eqtup1
  expr (unify (ret (tupv 1 2)) (ret (tupv 1 2)))
  norm (seq (ret 1) (seq (ret 2) (ret (tupv 1 2))))
  why "EqTup1 on a 2-element tuple: two elementwise unifications chained over the re-built tuple."

case eqtup2_length_mismatch
  head r_eqtup2
  expr (unify (ret (tupv unit)) (ret (tupv unit 2)))
  norm fail
  why "EqTup2: tuples of different lengths fail before any element is compared."

case eqfail_unit_vs_lam
  head r_eqfail
  expr (unify (ret unit) (ret (lam tany (glob 1))))
  norm fail
  why "EqFail: a literal against a lambda. The Fig. 9 no-rule exemption (lines 441-445) is the *same-root* case only."

case stuck_lam_vs_lam
  head none
  expr (unify (ret (lam tany (glob 1))) (ret (lam tany (glob 1))))
  norm (unify (ret (lam tany (glob 1))) (ret (lam tany (glob 1))))
  why "lambda vs lambda has no row at all (Fig. 9 lines 441-442): stuck, not fail."

case stuck_var_vs_var
  head none
  expr (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret (var 0)))
  norm (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret (var 0)))
  why "`x = x` is stuck until x gets a value (lines 444-445)."

case stuck_var_vs_value
  head none
  expr (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 3))
  norm (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 3))
  why "A bare variable on either side takes no Eq row (no_eq_rule_pair); binding `x` is the Fig. 8 promote/subst rows, and those only fire inside a `block` whose slot they can fill - outside a block there is no binder to bind, so `x = 3` stays stuck."

case eqfail_primhead_vs_int
  head r_eqfail
  expr (unify (ret (primv padd)) (ret 1))
  norm fail
  why "EqFail: prim head against a literal (different roots)."

case stuck_primhead_vs_primhead
  head none
  expr (unify (ret (primv padd)) (ret (primv psub)))
  norm (unify (ret (primv padd)) (ret (primv psub)))
  why "same-root exemption on prim heads (Fig. 10 note, line 463): no row, stuck."

case eqfail_delay_vs_int
  head r_eqfail
  expr (unify (ret (delay fail)) (ret 1))
  norm fail
  why "EqFail on a delay against a literal; `delay` vs `delay` is the same-root stuck case."

# ---------- Fig. 11: beta / bind rows ----------------------------------------

case betat0_empty_tuple
  head r_betat0
  expr (app (ret (tupv)) fail)
  norm fail
  why "BetaT0 base case: applying an empty tuple fails (RewBetaT0)."

# BetaL/BetaT *declare* a slot (`x{}`); since this close-out the Fig. 8 promote row
# fires on the `x = v` they create (docs/VERSE-RULES.md sec. 3.2, paper line 416), so a
# beta canonical form no longer stops at the unification - it reaches a value under a
# heap entry.  The `head` claims below are about the ROOT as written (still the App),
# the `norm` claims are the whole hand-derived walk.

case betal_declares_slot
  head r_betal
  expr (app (ret (lam tany (glob 1))) (ret unit))
  norm (block ((tany unit)) (glob 1))
  why "BetaL (line 468) -> `block x{}. (x = unit ; body)`; PromoteL (line 416) fires on that `x = unit` (slot 0 is declared-only, unit mentions no slot of this block, no other slot value mentions slot 0), fills slot 0 and replaces the unification by `ret unit`; the rebuild then takes R_Seq (line 483) and leaves the body. liftE does not touch Glob: a global name is index-neutral."

case betal_body_is_slot_variable
  head r_betal
  expr (app (ret (lam tany (ret (var 0)))) (ret 7))
  norm (block ((tany 7)) (ret 7))
  why "BetaL (line 468): the body keeps its indices, so the body's `var 0` names the freshly declared slot and the *argument* is lifted by the new binder. PromoteL (line 416) fills slot 0 with 7 and rewrites `x = 7` to `ret 7`; Subst (line 412) then reads the body's `var 0` as the slot value; R_Seq drops the left value. `(\x.x) 7` is now a value under a heap entry - the adequacy consequence of decision 3 (docs/RULES-AUDIT.md sec. 3, class C1)."

case betat_single_candidate
  head r_betat
  expr (app (ret (tupv unit)) (ret 3))
  norm (block ((tany 3)) (block () (seq (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 0)) (ret unit))))
  cands (block ((tany 3)) (block () (seq (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 0)) (ret unit))))
  why "BetaT (line 469) binds the argument once (line 474: `x = e` exists so e is not duplicated) and gives each element its own `block{}. (x = i ; v_i)`; a one-element candidate list IS that candidate (choice_list), so no spurious choice node. PromoteL fills the outer slot 0 with 3, R_Seq drops the binding. Inside the candidate `x = 0` is stuck: that block has no slots of its own, so its body's index 0 names the enclosing binder and the Subst context (`k : CtxS`) has no hole through a Block - audit record `Subst-Deep`."

case betat_two_candidates
  head r_betat
  expr (app (ret (tupv 1 2)) (ret 7))
  norm (block ((tany 7)) (choice (block () (seq (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 0)) (ret 1))) (block () (seq (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)) (ret 2)))))
  cands (block ((tany 7)) (block () (seq (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 0)) (ret 1)))) (block ((tany 7)) (block () (seq (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)) (ret 2))))
  why "BetaT with a 2-element tuple: choice_list folds the two candidates into one choice node - the candidate bag the denotation reads as br2 (T2). PromoteL fills the outer slot with 7, R_Seq drops the binding, and the choice rebuild normalizes both branches (each branch is a `Block nil`, so it is its own normal form here). All slots are valued, so cand_bag reads the block through and hands back two candidates, each carrying the heap it was produced under."

# ---------- Fig. 10: primops via the schema rows -----------------------------

case prim_add_two_args
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret 7) (ret 35)))
  norm (ret 42)
  why "add (line 452), dispatched by the Prim2 schema row (R_Prim stands in for the named rows Add/Gt1/IsInt1/Cons/DotDot, Eval.v head table)."

case prim_neg_bare_operand
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pneg)) (ret 3))
  norm (ret -3)
  why "A bare `ret v` operand unpacks to the one-element list (Semantics.v decision 5), matching the denotation's apply_c unpacking."

case prim_ppls_identity
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv ppls)) (ret 9))
  norm (ret 9)
  why "pls is the identity on an integer."

case prim_ppls_not_int
  head r_primfail
  expr (app (ret (primv ppls)) (ret unit))
  norm fail
  why "PPls has a catch-all fail row for a non-integer single argument."

case prim_gt_left_operand
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pgt)) (tup (ret 5) (ret 2)))
  norm (ret 5)
  why "gt1 (line 453): the guard holds and the prim returns its left operand."

case prim_gt_guard_fails
  head r_primfail
  expr (app (ret (primv pgt)) (tup (ret 2) (ret 5)))
  norm fail
  why "gt2 (line 454): the guard fails, so the primop fails."

case prim_pneq_equal
  head r_primfail
  expr (app (ret (primv pneq)) (tup (ret 4) (ret 4)))
  norm fail
  why "PNEq fails when the operands are equal (its `a != b` guard)."

case prim_isint_on_bool
  head r_primfail
  expr (app (ret (primv pisint)) (tup (ret true)))
  norm fail
  why "isInt2 (line 456)."

case prim_isbool_passthrough
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pisbool)) (ret true))
  norm (ret true)
  why "isBool returns the boolean it checked."

case prim_cons_tuple
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pcons)) (tup (ret unit) (ret (tupv 1 2))))
  norm (ret (tupv unit 1 2))
  why "cons (line 457) prepends onto a tuple value."

case prim_dotdot_range
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdotdot)) (tup (ret 1) (ret 3)))
  norm (ret (tupv 1 2 3))
  why "dotdot (paper line 459) is inclusive on both ends: `dotdot[ki1,ki2] -> ki1 (ki1+1) .. ki2`. Syntax.v materialises the range with seq_z, whose guard is `lo <= hi` and whose fuel is |hi-lo|+1 - exactly the inclusive length. This expectation is what caught an off-by-one in the kernel (docs/DESIGN-NOTES.md sec. 8)."

case prim_dotdot_single_element
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdotdot)) (tup (ret 2) (ret 2)))
  norm (ret (tupv 2))
  why "A one-point range `2..2` is the single element 2: the guard is `lo <= hi`, not `lo < hi`."

case prim_dotdot_descending_empty
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdotdot)) (tup (ret 5) (ret 2)))
  norm (ret (tupv))
  why "A descending range is empty, and an empty tuple is a value (RTup of zero elements), so the row still fires. The pinned oracle agrees: Haskell `[5..2] = []`."

case prim_arrlen
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv parrlen)) (tup (ret (tupv unit true 5))))
  norm (ret 3)
  why "arrLen reports the tuple length as an integer."

case prim_div_zero_is_stuck
  head none
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret 7) (ret 0)))
  norm (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret 7) (ret 0)))
  why "Division by zero is PStuck: no row fires and the term stays as it is. The pinned oracle has no Div clause for j = 0 either (Red.hs:1346)."

case prim_div_negative_floor
  head r_prim
  expr (app (ret (primv pdiv)) (tup (ret -7) (ret 2)))
  norm (ret -4)
  why "Z.div floors toward -infinity (-7 / 2 = -4, remainder 1); the pinned oracle uses Haskell `div`, which floors the same way (Red.hs:1347). Fig. 10 line 452 states `ki1 / ki2` without fixing the rounding for negative operands, so this case pins the kernel to the oracle."

case prim_add_wrong_value_shape
  head none
  expr (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret unit) (ret 1)))
  norm (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret unit) (ret 1)))
  why "PAdd has no row for a non-integer operand, so this is PStuck, not PFail: arity *and* value shape gate the row."

case prim_operand_not_a_value_tuple
  head none
  expr (app (ret (primv pmul)) (tup (ret 2) (seq (ret 2) (ret 3))))
  norm (app (ret (primv pmul)) (tup (ret 2) (ret 3)))
  why "prim_operand_vals rejects an operand list that is not `ret v` all the way down, so no row fires at this visit; the structural pass does normalize the operand (`seq 2 3` -> 3), and the now-fireable mul row waits for the next fuel pass (README sec. 3)."

# ---------- choice, blocks, and the deferred rows ----------------------------

case choice_has_no_row_but_normalizes_inside
  head none
  expr (choice (app (ret (primv padd)) (tup (ret 7) (ret 35))) (ret 1))
  norm (choice (ret 42) (ret 1))
  cands (ret 42) (ret 1)
  why "Candidate bags have no rewrite row in Figs. 9-12 (choice is the denotation's br2, T2), but the head table normalizes inside the branches. cand_bag reads the two branch values off the normal form - what a candidate bag *is* in M0, stated as a corpus claim and not as an implementation detail."

case block_slots_are_untouched
  head none
  expr (block ((tint) (tany unit)) (seq (ret true) fail))
  norm (block ((tint) (tany unit)) fail)
  why "A Block root fires only when the Fig. 8 walk finds a redex inside the body; here the body is `ret true ; fail` - a value and a Fail, with no slot variable to read and no declared slot to bind - so the walk selects nothing (HpAlpha/LamAlpha never fire either: de Bruijn renames trivially) and the body normalizes underneath by R_Seq while the slot list is left alone."

case iter_rows_never_selected
  head none
  expr (iter iall (ret (tupv 1)) (ret 2))
  norm (iter iall (ret (tupv 1)) (ret 2))
  why "Every Fig. 13 row matches `Iter ic (Block slots ...) e` - the body has to be a block - and Eval.v defers all of them anyway (they need the CtxC context-hole matcher; their soundness is the admitted family, docs/RULES-AUDIT.md sec. 5). Here the head table selects nothing and the branches are values, so the term is its own normal form."

case iter_all_shape_matches_but_deferred
  head none
  expr (iter iall (block ((tint)) (ret 1)) (ret 2))
  norm (iter iall (block ((tint)) (ret 1)) (ret 2))
  why "This shape is exactly RewIterAll's left-hand side (iter(all){B[v]}{e} -> cons[B[v], e], Fig. 13 line 512), but the head table defers the whole iter family: those rows need the CtxC context-hole matcher, so the kernel selects no redex here and the term is its own normal form. The slot list is untouched by normalization (Block's slots are not subterms the evaluator descends into)."

case iter_if_value_operand_is_stuck
  head none
  expr (iter iif (ret 1) (ret 2))
  norm (iter iif (ret 1) (ret 2))
  why "Same deferral, with a value on the iter body: RewIterIf matches the `if x = delay ...` shape only, and no iter row is in Eval.v's head table at all, so nothing fires anywhere in this term."

# ---------- Fig. 8: slot completion at a Block root (Subst / PromoteL / PromoteR) --
# These rows fire *inside* a block body: `head_redex (Block slots body)` searches that
# body through the two binder-free context grammars the ctors quantify over (CtxS for
# Subst, CtxC for the promote rows), so the redex it reports is the ctor's own
# right-hand side (KernelTest.v section D proves the coincidence).  Order inside one
# body: PromoteL, then PromoteR, then Subst-at-node, then the descent - a kernel
# strategy decision, recorded in docs/DESIGN-NOTES.md sec. 9, not a paper claim.
# Slot index rule: body index i names slot p = n-1-i exactly when i < n.

case subst_valued_slot
  head r_subst
  expr (block ((tint 5)) (ret (var 0)))
  norm (block ((tint 5)) (ret 5))
  cands (block ((tint 5)) (ret 5))
  cands_why "one candidate: the slot list is complete, so the block is read through and the candidate carries it"
  why "Subst (line 412): `exists X{hp, x v}. S[x] -> exists X{hp, x v}. S[v]`. Body index 0 is slot 1-1-0 = 0 and its heap entry is the value 5, so the row fires with S = the empty context; slotValueFromBody 1 0 5 = 5 (a closed value needs no reindexing). The slot list itself is untouched - Subst reads the heap, it does not change it."

case subst_inside_seq
  head r_subst
  expr (block ((tint 5)) (seq (ret (var 0)) (ret 1)))
  norm (block ((tint 5)) (ret 1))
  why "The walk descends through `S ; e` - the ctor's context is k = HS_SeqR HS_Hole (ret 1), so the Subst redex is the left leaf - and head steps are re-visited, so R_Seq (line 483) drops the substituted value in the next pass and leaves `ret 1`."

case subst_declared_slot_needs_a_value
  head none
  expr (block ((tany)) (ret (var 0)))
  norm (block ((tany)) (ret (var 0)))
  why "Subst's premise is `x v` - a *valued* heap entry - and the promote rows need a unification to bind, so a lone declared slot takes no row: stuck, not failed. That is decision 3's SStuck, the C1 boundary docs/RULES-AUDIT.md sec. 3 records."

case subst_then_eqfail
  head r_subst
  expr (block ((tint 5)) (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 7)))
  norm (block ((tint 5)) fail)
  why "PromoteL/R cannot fire (their premise is `exists X,x{hp}`, a declared-only slot), so the walk descends into the left leaf and Subst fires: `x = 7` becomes `5 = 7`, the EqK2 pair (line 434), which fails under the still-valued slot."

case subst_then_eqfail_from_the_right_side
  head r_subst
  expr (block ((tint 5)) (unify (ret 1) (ret (var 0))))
  norm (block ((tint 5)) fail)
  why "The same row reached through the *right* side of the unification: the descent tries e1 first (nothing there), then e2. PromoteR is tried at the node and blocked by the valued slot, so Subst fires; `1 = 5` is EqK2."

case guard_blocked_promote_still_descends
  head r_subst
  expr (block ((tint 5)) (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret (var 0))))
  norm (block ((tint 5)) (ret 5))
  why "Both promote directions match this node syntactically and both fail their guard (the slot is valued, not declared). Coq does not fall through to the next arm after a guard failure, so the kernel must (and does) descend instead: Subst fires on the left leaf, then on the right one, and the EqK1 pair (line 432) closes `5 = 5`. This case pins that shape."

case promotel_binds_declared_slot
  head r_promotel
  expr (block ((tany)) (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret 7)))
  norm (block ((tany 7)) (ret 7))
  why "PromoteL (line 416): `exists X,x{hp}. C[x = v] -> exists X,x{hp[v/x], x v}. C[v]`. The kernel's M0 reading fills slot 0 with 7 and restricts hp[v/x] to the identity (promote_hp_ok); the ctor's rhs is `fillC k (Ret v)`, so the unification node is *replaced by the value* - which is what turns a beta canonical form from SStuck into a value."

case promoter_var_on_right
  head r_promoter
  expr (block ((tany)) (unify (ret 7) (ret (var 0))))
  norm (block ((tany 7)) (ret 7))
  why "PromoteR (line 417, 'just like promote-l'): the value may sit on either side, and the kernel tries the two directions as two independent matches rather than as two arms of one match, so a left side that is not in slot range cannot shadow the right-side match. Paper lines 425-428 are why PromoteR is a row and not a swap rule."

case promote_blocked_by_another_slot
  head none
  expr (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret (var 1))))
  norm (block ((tany) (tany)) (unify (ret (var 0)) (ret (var 1))))
  why "`x = y` with both sides locally bound: body index 0 is slot 1 and index 1 is slot 0, so each side's value mentions this block's own slots and promote_ok (fvsol(v) # dom(hp) union {x}, line 416) blocks both directions; Subst needs a valued slot and neither is one. This is the pair the paper refuses to swap (lines 425-428) because it could loop."

case promote_blocked_by_an_existing_heap_ref
  head none
  expr (block ((tany) (tint (var 0))) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret 7)))
  norm (block ((tany) (tint (var 0))) (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret 7)))
  why "Slot 1's value `var 0` names slot 0 (a slot value sees its own prefix at index q-1-p), so binding slot 0 would rewrite an *existing* entry: that is `hp[v/x]`, and the M0 restriction promote_hp_ok (identity-only) blocks the row. The general evolving-heap form is the tier-2 half of PromoteL/R, discharged at M3/T1."

case promote_then_subst_two_slots
  head r_promotel
  expr (block ((tany) (tint 9)) (seq (unify (ret (var 1)) (ret 7)) (ret (var 0))))
  norm (block ((tany 7) (tint 9)) (ret 9))
  why "Index arithmetic over two slots: index 1 of a 2-slot block is slot 0 (declared -> PromoteL fires), index 0 is slot 1 (valued -> Subst fires on the *next* visit, reading slot 1 from the body PromoteL just rewrote). R_Seq then drops the left value. Subst and the promote rows never compete for the same slot - only for the same position."

case subst_no_hole_through_a_nested_block
  head none
  expr (block ((tint 5)) (block () (ret (var 0))))
  norm (block ((tint 5)) (block () (ret (var 0))))
  why "The Subst context (CtxS, Syntax.v) has no hole through a Block - a nested binder is not a slot of the outer block - so the walk stops at the inner block, and inside it index 0 is out of range (n = 0 slots). The pinned oracle's deep substitution (Red.hs:1059) would rewrite here; this case pins the kernel's narrowing, which docs/RULES-AUDIT.md sec. 2 records as `Subst-Deep`. It is a *deviation record*, not a claim that the oracle agrees."

case beta_choice_two_candidates
  head none
  expr (choice (app (ret (lam tany (ret 3))) (ret 7)) (ret 1))
  norm (choice (block ((tany 7)) (ret 3)) (ret 1))
  cands (block ((tany 7)) (ret 3)) (ret 1)
  cands_why "two candidates: a choice node splits, and each candidate keeps the heap it was produced under"
  why "Choice has no rewrite row (Figs. 9-12), so the root takes no row while both branches normalize: BetaL then PromoteL then R_Seq on the left, nothing to do on the right. The two branch normal forms are the candidates T2's br2 models."

case cand_bag_choice_under_a_bind_is_one_candidate
  head none
  expr (seq (choice (ret 3) (ret 1)) (ret 7))
  norm (seq (choice (ret 3) (ret 1)) (ret 7))
  cands (seq (choice (ret 3) (ret 1)) (ret 7))
  cands_why "one candidate: cand_bag does not move a choice across a bind - that move is a *rule*, not a reading"
  why "No row fires (`Seq (Ret v) e` needs a value on the left; Norm5 needs a Seq on the left), so the rebuild normalizes the children and the term is its own normal form. cand_bag reports ONE candidate here: splitting `x <- (a|b); k x` into `(x<-a;k x) | (x<-b;k x)` is the bind/br2 interchange law, i.e. IterChoice's deferred ctrees-side content (docs/RULES-AUDIT.md sec. 4). A cand_bag that split under Seq/= would be asserting a law the kernel has no rules for."

case cand_bag_declared_block_is_a_leaf
  head none
  expr (block ((tany)) (choice (ret 3) (ret 1)))
  norm (block ((tany)) (choice (ret 3) (ret 1)))
  cands (block ((tany)) (choice (ret 3) (ret 1)))
  cands_why "one candidate: a block with a declared-only slot is decision 3's SStuck, so it is a leaf, not a bag"
  why "The body walk has no redex (Choice is a barrier for the Subst/promote contexts) and the rebuild normalizes the body, which is already a choice of two values. cand_bag agrees with the denotation here: `all_slots_valued` is false, so the block is one leaf - the same reading closeSlots gives it."
